<h2 id="step-1-check-your-free-space-212-gb" tabindex="-1">Step 1: Check your free space (212 GB+)</h2>
<ol>
<li>Press the <strong>PS button</strong>, then open <strong>Settings</strong>.</li>
<li>Select <strong>Storage</strong>.</li>
<li>Check you have <strong>more than 212 GB</strong> free.</li>
</ol>
<p>![PS5 storage screen](TODO: image:ps5-settings-step-2 — menu screen, PS5: Settings &gt; Storage with free space visible)
<em>Caption: Menu screen. The PS5 storage screen showing free space.</em></p>
<h2 id="step-2-set-up-hdr-general-ps5-tip-not-a-gta-6-setting" tabindex="-1">Step 2: Set up HDR (general PS5 tip, not a GTA 6 setting)</h2>
<ol>
<li>Open <strong>Settings</strong>.</li>
<li>Select <strong>Screen and Video</strong>.</li>
<li>Select <strong>Video Output</strong>.</li>
<li>Select <strong>Adjust HDR</strong>.</li>
<li>Follow the on-screen steps.</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="step-3-pick-the-right-tv-settings" tabindex="-1">Step 3: Pick the right TV settings</h2>
<ol>
<li>Open your TV’s picture menu.</li>
<li>Turn on <strong>Game Mode</strong> (name varies by TV). This lowers delay between button press and picture.</li>
<li>120 Hz will <strong>not</strong> make GTA 6 faster. The game targets 30 FPS.</li>
<li>VRR (a setting that smooths the picture on some TVs): GTA 6 support is not announced. Leave it on <strong>Automatic</strong> for now.</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="step-4-change-the-controller-buzz" tabindex="-1">Step 4: Change the controller buzz</h2>
<ol>
<li>Start GTA 6 once it unlocks.</li>
<li>Open the game’s <strong>Settings</strong> and look for <strong>Accessibility</strong>.</li>
<li>Change the haptics (buzz) and adaptive triggers (stiff buttons) there. Rockstar says they are adjustable in accessibility settings. The exact menu names are not announced yet.</li>
</ol>
<p>![DualSense controller diagram with haptics and trigger labels](TODO: image:ps5-settings-step-3 — diagram, our own art: a controller outline labelling haptics, adaptive triggers, speaker)
<em>Caption: Diagram. The DualSense parts GTA 6 uses.</em></p>
<h2 id="xbox-series-x-s-what-is-known" tabindex="-1">Xbox Series X|S: what is known</h2>
<ul>
<li>Xbox Series X and Series S run it natively. Rockstar gave no Xbox-only graphics options.</li>
<li><strong>Xbox Cloud Gaming</strong> (streaming) uses <strong>Xbox Series S graphics settings</strong>, per Rockstar and Xbox Wire.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="not-announced-we-will-update-at-launch" tabindex="-1">Not announced (we will update at launch)</h2>
<ul>
<li>Performance mode on PS5 Pro</li>
<li>Resolution on PS5 and PS5 Pro</li>
<li>HDR, motion blur, field-of-view and camera options in the game</li>
<li>Launch-day update size</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="faq" tabindex="-1">FAQ</h2>
<h3 id="does-gta-6-run-at-60-fps-on-ps5" tabindex="-1">Does GTA 6 run at 60 FPS on PS5?</h3>
<p>No. Rockstar targets 30 FPS. No 60 FPS mode is announced.</p>
<h3 id="what-does-ps5-pro-add" tabindex="-1">What does PS5 Pro add?</h3>
<p>PSSR (sharper picture) and ray-traced shadows.</p>
<h3 id="how-much-space-do-i-need" tabindex="-1">How much space do I need?</h3>
<p>212 GB or more.</p>
<h3 id="is-there-a-performance-mode" tabindex="-1">Is there a performance mode?</h3>
<p>Not announced.</p>
<h3 id="can-i-change-the-controller-buzz" tabindex="-1">Can I change the controller buzz?</h3>
<p>Yes. Rockstar says haptics are adjustable in accessibility settings.</p>
